<section class="blk">
  <h2>The Transvaal, before anyone thought this family was there</h2>
  <p class="dek">Three notices found by reading a surname rather than chasing a reference. The first of
    them is the oldest document in this archive.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:10rem">Reference</th><th style="width:17rem">Person</th><th class="m">Died</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">Sterfkennis 678<br /><span class="cap">Transvaal, Weeskamer</span></td>
          <td><strong>Petrus Jacobus Booijsen</strong> &mdash; generation six, b. Graaff-Reinet c. 1788</td><td class="m">1876</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Read</span> <b>Parents named</b>: Gerthardus L&ouml;dewikus Booijsen and Magdalena de Beer. Eight children. Nothing to leave.
            <a href={u("/documents/#sterfkennis-1876")}>Transcribed &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Sterfkennis 6332</td>
          <td><strong>Anna Johanna Booijzen, born Alberts</strong> &mdash; b. district Cradock c. 1823</td><td class="m">1891</td>
          <td><span class="chip inf">Read, kinship unproven</span> Died Boekfontein, district Rustenburg. Parents Ocken Johannes Alberts and Anna Johanna Botha.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Sterfkennis 44420</td>
          <td><strong>Magdalena Susanna Maria van den Berg, born Booyzen</strong> &mdash; b. c. 1860</td><td class="m">1920</td>
          <td><span class="chip inf">Read, kinship unproven</span> Died Vaalbank, district Lichtenburg. Father entered as <b>Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus Booyzen</b>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>How they were found matters more than what they say.</b> The nine
    references below came from NAAIRS, one search at a time, and this archive spent days working through
    them. These three came from asking FamilySearch's Transvaal probate collection for
    <span class="m">every indexed BOOYSEN and BOOYZEN at once</span> and reading the list. That took
    minutes. The lesson is recorded on <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was searched</a>, because it is the
    method that failed, not the archive.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The Transvaal estates — located, and mostly still unread</h2>
  <p class="dek">Found only after this archive noticed it had spent its life searching the Cape depot
    alone. Every reference below is in hand; <b>three have been read</b>, and one of the three arrived
    unlooked-for.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m" style="width:10rem">Reference</th><th style="width:17rem">Person</th><th class="m">Died</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 2428/40</td><td><strong>Stephanus Francois Booyzen</strong> — son of the second marriage</td><td class="m">1940</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Read</span> A mason. Parents given as <em>"oorlede"</em>.
            <a href={u("/documents/")}>Transcribed &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 2945/44</td><td><strong>Anna Johanna Maria Booyzen, born Pretorius</strong> — indexed under her husband as "Spouse"</td><td class="m">1944</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Read</span> Both parents named, with a Germiston address. Six children, all minors.
            <a href={u("/documents/")}>Transcribed &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 1928/42</td><td><strong>Joseph Heinrich Daschner</strong> — her husband, who married her at Indwe</td><td class="m">1942</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Read</span> Both parents named. Eleven children, five carrying Booyzen and Barry names.
            <a href={u("/documents/")}>Transcribed &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 3734/46</td><td><strong>Catherine Mary Sophia Daschner, born Booyzen</strong> — b. 22 Apr 1891</td><td class="m">1946</td>
          <td>Located · <b>not name-indexed</b> — needs film browsing</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 1095/48</td><td><strong>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen</strong></td><td class="m">1947</td>
          <td>Located · <span class="m">ark 1:1:QGXP-3MKR</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 5155/51</td><td><strong>Gert Jacobus Booyzen</strong> — b. 19 Jan 1870</td><td class="m">1951</td><td>Located</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 769/52</td><td><strong>John Barry Booyzen</strong> — who signed the 1905 form</td><td class="m">1952</td><td>Located</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 10/57</td><td><strong>Henry James Booyzen</strong></td><td class="m">1956</td><td>Located</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG 52653</td><td><strong>Jacobus Nichols Booyzen</strong> — eldest on the 1863 list</td><td class="m">1923</td><td>Located</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">TAB MHG O/2899</td><td><strong>Jan Gerhardus Lodewikus Booyzen</strong> — seventh on that list</td><td class="m">1885</td><td>Located</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">The Transvaal probate collection is digitised, so these are readable rather
    than orderable — each should carry a <b>Sterfkennis</b> naming the deceased's parents, occupation and
    children. Between them they would settle what the men of this family did for a living in
    Johannesburg, which is the last untested line of the family testimony.</p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Reading them is rate-limited rather than blocked: FamilySearch
    began refusing requests partway through this work and recovers after a rest. The references are
    recorded here so the job resumes rather than restarts.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>And one thing learned about the route in.</b> The whole indexed surname
    can be pulled from one collection at a time &mdash; <span class="m">BOOYZEN, exact, collection
    2520237</span> returns 150 people &mdash; which is how the 1942 Daschner file was found without
    knowing it existed. It also settles a negative: <b>the 1946 to 1957 estates in this table are not in
    that index at all.</b> They exist on film and were never keyed, so the remaining six have to be
    reached by browsing images rather than by searching names, and that is a different and slower job.
    <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was searched &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Indexes searched, including where they returned nothing</h2>
  <p class="dek">A negative result is only worth recording if the search behind it is stated.</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Index</th><th>Query</th><th class="m">Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td rowspan="7"><strong>NAAIRS</strong> — National Automated Archival Information Retrieval
          System, <em>Cape Town Archives Repository (KAB)</em></td><td class="m">MOUNTJOY</td><td class="m">42</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOUNTJOY, beginning date ≥ 1930</td><td class="m">22 — all read</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MONTJOY</td><td class="m">4 — all read</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOUNTJOY <b>and</b> HERKLAAS</td><td class="m">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOUNTJOY <b>and</b> HERCULES</td><td class="m">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOUNTJOY <b>and</b> WODEHOUSE</td><td class="m">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">MOUNTJOY <b>and</b> BARKLY</td><td class="m">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>FamilySearch</strong> full-text search</td><td class="m">"Johannes Mountjoy"</td>
          <td class="m">0 — the collection does not cover Cape probate</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><strong>What the zeros mean here.</strong> There is <b>no estate file for
    Hercules Johannes Mountjoy</b> (d. 12 May 1941, Wodehouse) in the Cape Town Archives under any
    spelling. The estates around him run 1938, 1939, 1943, 1946 and 1948 — an unbroken sequence — so this
    is an <em>absence</em>, not a hole in the index. His parentage is instead carried by his
    <b>1866 baptism register entry</b>, which is better evidence than a death notice would have been.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:12px" class="cap"><strong>One correction to an external source.</strong> The
    FamilySearch ark cited by <em>1820settlers.com</em> for James Jonathan Mountjoy
    (<span class="m">3:1:3Q9M-CSQ8-S3QM-6</span>) is not him. It is the death notice of
    <b>Ferdinand Kumm</b>, d. 20 October 1903 at Stutterheim — his father-in-law.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Published and reference sources</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><b>Forebears</b> — surname incidence and distribution for <em>Booyzen</em> and <em>Booysen</em>,
      accessed 9 September 2026. Forebears figures are modelled estimates from name-frequency data, not a
      census. <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
    <p><b>Wikipedia</b>, <em>Booysen</em> — surname etymology and the progenitor Boij Boijeszoon of Barlt,
      Northern Friesland.</p>
    <p><b>Hilary Anne Shearing</b>, <em>The Cape Rebel of the South African War, 1899–1902</em>
      (Stellenbosch University) — the standard study; the source of the rebel classification system and the
      scale of the rebellion.</p>
    <p><b>The Van Plettenberg Historical Society</b>, "Cape Rebels 1899–1902" — district rebel numbers,
      trial and sentencing outcomes, the 44 executions and the 409 transportations.</p>
    <p><b>angloboerwar.com</b> — Barkly East and Dordrecht rebel numbers, Commandant D. Schoeman at
      Labuschagne's Nek, Commandant J. F. de Wet and the Barkly East Rebels.</p>
    <p><b>Anglo-Boer War Museum, Bloemfontein — wmbr.org.za</b>, searched 9 September 2026. Fifteen
      registers, including <b>Cape Rebels (12,792)</b>, <b>Joiners (952)</b>, <b>Farm List (10,282)</b>
      and <b>Boer Officers (514)</b>. Records cited here: <span class="m">Cape Rebels ID 8434</span>
      (Schoeman, Daniel Willem, Caerlaverock) and <span class="m">ID 4721</span> (Klopper, Christiaan F.,
      Caerlaverock). <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

